  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe slow moving low pressure system drifting over the Southwest\ncontinues to pump tropical moisture across the region. Water\nlogged soils and bank full rivers continue to pose a flash flood\nrisk. Many rivers have begun to recede today. However, portions\nof the San Juan and Dolores Rivers will see additional runoff \nfrom smaller basins impact their flows through this evening.\nFlood Watches and Warnings were left active until this runoff\nthreat has safely passed. An area of convection continues to\ntrain northward along the state line. This has produced some\nsteep precipitation rates in a few isolated storms. Hi res\nmodels are keen to keep this activity going into the overnight\nhours as the low begins to lift northeast towards the Four\nCorners. While some drier air begins to wrap into the low, the\nthreat of flash flooding from training storms will continue\novernight due to the vulnerability of saturated watersheds in \nsoutheast UT and southwest CO. A few rivers that receded today \ncould see a return upward as additional runoff increases their \nflows. Snow levels drop to around 9 to 10K overnight, which \ncould leave some snow on mountain passes at road level.\n\nThe low will finally begin to lift out of the region on Sunday.\nForcing along the frontal boundary is expected to produce\nanother round of showers and thunderstorms. Some drier air noses\ninto the region from the southwest. This is expected to chip\naway at rain rates, but a few productive thunderstorms with\nstronger rainfall could easily overwhelm this dry signature.\nWhile severe storms are not expected, a few strong storms will\nbe capable of strong winds, hail, and torrential rainfall, given\nthe frontal dynamics on Sunday. Models eject the low out of the\nRockies by early Monday morning. Colder air wrapped behind this\nsystem will keep some snow flying on our mountain passes early\nMonday. Commuters and travelers on Rabbit Ears and Vail Passes\ncan expect some snow early Monday. Accumulations don't look to\nbe a problem, but motorists should keep abreast of forecast\nupdates.\n\nDrier conditions and broad zonal flow develop Monday and Tuesday\nacross the West. A frontal boundary draped across the West will\ndeliver a weak clipper wave to our northern counties, including\nsome light rain and snow for the Uintas and northern mountains\nof CO. A storm digging south out of British Columbia on\nWednesday will send heights upward, as southwest flow returns\nacross the Four Corners. Temperatures return to near normal\nunder this warmer regime Wednesday and Thursday. Models are\nintroducing some stronger winds late next week too, as another\nsystem develops. This system will potentially bring another\nround of active weather to the region by next weekend.",
